efines “dose adjustment” as an ongoing process of changing thyroid hormone dose based on TSH blood test results to achieve target TSH levels and minimize side effects/risks, reinforcing the continuous nature of management. [Data: Entities (5057, 4999, 4996); Relationships (11075, 11011)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Total thyroidectomy is positioned as a standard, higher-risk cancer option and directly causes lifelong dependence on hormone replacement\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Total thyroidectomy is described as removal of the entire thyroid gland, typically under general anesthesia via a small neck incision, and is characterized as a standard treatment option most often recommended for higher-risk thyroid cancers (including papillary cancers that have grown/spread beyond the thyroid), large tumors (generally >4 cm), and suspected follicular or oncocytic tumors that may extend outside the thyroid. [Data: Entities (5021); Relationships (11058)]\\n\\nThe data also ties treatment planning to extent of disease: spread to “distant areas” beyond the thyroid/local neck region is cited as a factor supporting recommendation for total thyroidectomy. This indicates that concern for distant spread can shift management toward more extensive surgery. [Data: Entities (5118, 5021); Relationships (11309)]\\n\\nBecause the entire thyroid is removed, the dataset states patients require lifelong thyroid hormone replacement after total thyroidectomy. This dependence is also consistent with the broader description of thyroid hormones: after a total thyroidectomy, thyroid hormones are no longer produced naturally and therefore must be replaced with medication. [Data: Entities (5021, 4995, 5026); Relationships (11058)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Levothyroxine-based thyroid hormone replacement is central post-surgery, with explicit TSH-targeting intent\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Thyroid hormone replacement therapy is described as a long-term medication treatment u